              },
              {
                  icon: "/assets/images/units/abilities/movement_augment_256.webp",
                  title: "Movement",
                  text: "+1 or +2 movement steps. Sometimes one extra cell is worth more than another damage buff.",
              },
          ],
          doctrinesTitle: "Doctrines: what you see costs you points",
          doctrinesIntro:
              "Your doctrine is the very first pick of the draft, and it lasts the whole draft. It decides how much of the opponent's roster you watch — and it pays you for looking away: the less you see, the larger your augment budget. There is no fourth option and you cannot change it later.",
          doctrinesTableCaption: "Doctrines: reveal versus augment points",
          doctrinesColumns: ["Doctrine", "What you see", "Augment points"],
          doctrines: [
              {
                  name: "Spymaster",
                  sees: "Every one of the opponent's picks, as they happen.",
                  points: 5,
                  note: "Full information, smallest army budget.",
              },
              {
                  name: "Scout",
                  sees: "One random level-1 slot, one level-2 slot, and one of the two senior slots (level 3 or 4).",
                  points: 6,
                  note: "A partial read for a middling budget.",
              },
              {
                  name: "Battle Trance",
                  sees: "Nothing. The opponent's roster stays hidden until the fight.",
                  points: 7,
                  note: "Draft blind, field the strongest army.",
              },
          ],
          doctrinesOutro:
              "Those points are the whole augment budget you spend before placement, so the doctrine is an army-size decision as much as an information one. Picking a creature the opponent already took simply sends you back to pick again — unless your doctrine revealed that slot.",
          synergiesTitle: "Faction synergies: 2, 4, and 6 units",
          synergiesIntro:
              "When you field 2/4/6 units from the same faction, that faction reaches synergy level 1/2/3. Each faction has two possible bonuses, and there is nothing to pick: which one a match fields is drawn from the game id — an even 50/50 per faction, drawn independently, identical for you and your opponent, and shown on the rails from the first screen of the draft.",
          synergyLevels: "Power by level",
          synergies: [
              {
                  faction: "Life",
                  icon: "/assets/images/units/factions/life_128.webp",
                  options: ["Supply: +6% / +12% / +19% bodies in each stack", "Morale & luck: +6/+2, +13/+5, +20/+9"],
              },
              {
                  faction: "Chaos",
                  icon: "/assets/images/units/factions/chaos_128.webp",
                  options: ["Movement: +1 / +2 / +3 cells", "Break on attack: 6% / 11% / 17% chance to shut off abilities"],
              },
              {
                  faction: "Might",
                  icon: "/assets/images/units/factions/might_128.webp",
                  options: ["Aura range: +1 / +2 / +3 cells", "Stack abilities power: +5% / +8% / +12%"],
              },
              {
                  faction: "Nature",
                  icon: "/assets/images/units/factions/nature_128.webp",
                  options: ["Board units: +2 / +3 / +4 fielded stacks", "Flying armor: +15% / +30% / +50%"],
              },
          ],
          placementTitle: "Placement: many fights are won before the first hit",
          placementParagraphs: [
              "Choose a unit from the roster, set the stack amount, and place it in your allowed deployment zone. You can move placed stacks, clone the selected stack, delete it, and tune the final shape before starting the fight.",
              "Large units occupy 2x2 cells. They are powerful pieces, but they ask for space: harder to route through a crowded center and harder to save when narrowing starts.",
              "Before pressing start, check three things: who is inside your auras, who can be reached on the first turn, and where your ranged units will stand if the enemy dives them.",
          ],
          turnsTitle: "Turns: one decision, then the queue moves",
          turnActions: [
              {
                  title: "Move",
                  text: "The path must be legal and fit inside the unit's Steps. Moving closer to the nearest enemy raises morale; backing away can lower it.",
              },
              {
                  title: "Melee attack",
                  text: "The unit must stand next to the target. A ranged unit forced into melee hits at reduced damage unless an ability covers it.",
              },
              {
                  title: "Ranged attack",
                  text: "You need shots, a valid line, and no melee lock. Long shots are reduced by a range divisor up to x8 unless Sniper avoids the penalty.",
              },
              {
                  title: "Spellbook",
                  text: "Spellbook units cast buffs, debuffs, healing, mass effects, summons, and position tricks. Three separate resistances gate effects: magic resist (magic damage + spell debuffs), status resist (Stun/Paralysis chance), and mind resist (full immunity to mind effects).",
              },
              {
                  title: "Hourglass",
                  text: "The unit waits and comes back later in the queue. It costs morale, but it can turn a bad action now into a better action later.",
              },
              {
                  title: "Luck Shield / Skip",
                  text: "Luck Shield (defend) clears this turn's random luck swing and ends the turn, for a small morale cost. A forced skip also ends the turn and costs morale; ending your turn normally after acting costs nothing.",
              },
          ],
          combatTitle: "Combat, without the formula sheet",
          combatParagraphs: [
              "Damage depends on attack, living bodies in the stack, target armor, luck, range distance, selected attack type, and active abilities. That is why the same unit can hit very differently from two different board states.",
              "Responses exist. Melee and ranged units can answer an attack if they have not already replied, are not blocked by an effect, and can actually reach the attacker.",
              "Abilities give fights their character: Lucky Strike can spike damage, Stun and Blindness break tempo, Petrifying Gaze can finish a target, Fire Shield returns damage, and Range Null Field shuts down shooting around it.",
          ],
          moraleTitle: "Morale, luck, and stack power",
          moraleItems: [
              "Morale runs from -20 to +20. Each lap a unit rolls about its morale value in percent to act an extra time (positive morale) or be skipped (negative morale). High morale also nudges movement range and, when it procs, boosts attack; low morale does the opposite.",
              "To build morale, advance toward the enemy army (+3 per move) and kill enemy stacks (a big push for the attacker). Retreating costs morale (-3), and when one of your stacks dies, your other stacks of that same unit lose morale too.",
              "Luck runs from -10 to +10 and is mostly defensive: a lucky stack takes less damage, an unlucky one takes more (about 1% per point). Your own luck also makes your abilities stronger and more likely to fire. Luck gets a small random swing each lap; Luck Shield (defend) clears that swing for a small morale cost.",
              "Stack power is recalculated from a stack's board value: unit experience times living amount, compared with the largest stack present. It ranges from 1 to 5.",
          ],
          moraleBreakdownTitle: "Exact morale changes",
          moraleBreakdown: [
              { change: "+3", text: "Move toward the nearest enemy" },
              { change: "+4", text: "Kill an enemy stack (goes to the attacking stack)" },
              { change: "-3", text: "Move away from the nearest enemy" },
              { change: "-4", text: "Each surviving stack of yours of a unit whose stack was wiped out" },
              { change: "-3", text: "Hourglass (Wait) to act later in the queue" },
              { change: "-2", text: "Defend (Luck Shield)" },
              { change: "-1", text: "Skip the turn, or let the turn timer run out" },
              { change: "±20", text: "Hard cap — morale never leaves this range" },
          ],
          moraleBreakdownNote:
              "Ending your turn normally after acting costs nothing. Each lap a unit rolls about its morale value in percent: a positive hit lets it act an extra time (and hit a little harder), a negative hit gets it skipped.",
          mapTitle: "The map does not wait forever",
          mapParagraphs: [
              "The board is a 16x16 grid. Ranked rolls one of three maps the moment the draft is created — each equally likely, one chance in three — and reveals it to both players mid-draft. The fight is then played on exactly that map; it is never re-rolled at the start of combat.",
          ],
          mapsTableCaption: "The three ranked maps",
          mapsColumns: ["Map", "Center", "Narrows", "Chance"],
          maps: [
              {
                  name: "Standard",
                  center: "Open field — no central hazard.",
                  narrows: "every 3 laps",
                  chance: "1 in 3",
              },
              {
                  name: "Lava",
                  center: "A lava pool scars the center of the board.",
                  narrows: "every 3 laps",
                  chance: "1 in 3",
              },
              {
                  name: "Cemetery",
                  center: "9 to 12 tombstones scattered at random across the middle four rows — the count is rolled per match too, and there is no single central block.",
                  narrows: "every 4 laps",
                  chance: "1 in 3",
              },
          ],
          mapsOutro:
              "A fourth map, Water, exists in the engine but is disabled in ranked, so you will never be dealt it. Narrowing turns outer cells into holes and pushes units inward — or kills them when there is no room. If neither army closes distance or deals damage, the engine can add extra narrowing pressure.",
          mapMechanicsTitle: "Two map rules worth knowing before you draft",
          mapMechanics: [
              {
                  title: "Lava is not walkable by default",
                  text: "Nothing may enter the central lava on its own. The Tier-2 artifact Lava Striders changes that for your entire army: your units may move over and stand in lava, and while they stand in central lava they gain Made of Fire — +10% to all stats, abilities included. On a Lava map that turns the hazard into ground only you can use.",
              },
              {
                  title: "Shooting through the Cemetery",
                  text: "Tombstones are structures, and an ordinary ranged attack is stopped by the first one in its lane. Double Shot spends up to two of its projectiles clearing stones instead of hitting the target. Cyclops (Large Caliber) and Gargantuan (Area Throw) ignore structures outright — their shots pass straight through and destroy the stones caught in the blast, which is what makes them the answer to a Cemetery lane.",
              },
          ],
          mapArmageddon:
              "After lap 12, Armageddon starts: four escalating damage waves gradually force a stalled fight to end.",
          examplesTitle: "Role cheat sheet",
          examples: [
              "Black Dragon and Angel show what large flying finishers can do, but both need room for a 2x2 body.",
              "Medusa, Arbalester, and Gargantuan teach firing lanes, ammo, and range distance.",
              "Satyr and Ogre Mage are reminders that a spellbook can matter more than raw damage.",
              "Pegasus, Griffin, Peasant, and Valkyrie prove that aura placement wins fights before the first attack lands.",
          ],
          finalTitle: "How to play better next match",
          finalItems: [
              "Start with a faction plan: which 2/4/6 units unlock the synergy you actually want?",
              "Place ranged units so the enemy's first dive does not shut off their shooting.",
              "Do not press Hourglass automatically. Wait only when you know what better turn you are creating.",
              "Track the narrowing map from lap one, not when the edge is already a hole.",
          ],
      };

const SectionTitle = embedded ? "h3" : "h2";
const ItemTitle = embedded ? "h4" : "h3";
const augmentDescriptions = new Map(copy.upgrades.map((upgrade) => [upgrade.icon, upgrade.text]));
const guideNavigation = isRu
    ? [
          {
              label: "Начало",
